    "chunk_size_bytes": 2011,
    "chunk_text": "(a)licensed commercial banks operating in Sri Lanka;\n\n(b)the Government and agencies or institutions actingon behalf of the Government, whether establishedby any written law or otherwise;\n\n(c)foreign commercial and investment banks;\n\n(e)international financial institutions; and\n\n(f)foreign Governments, foreign provincialgovernments and agencies or institutions actingon behalf of foreign Governments.\n\nForeignexchangeoperations ofthe CentralBank.\n\n30Central Bank of Sri Lanka\n\n5\n\n10\n\n15\n\n20\n\n25\n\n39.The Central Bank shall not accept any commissionor impose any charge of any description in respect of thepurchase or sale of foreign exchange, except telegraphic orother costs which are actually incurred in connection withsuch purchase or sale.\n\n40.(1) The Central Bank may grant collateralized loansto, or take loans from, any institution of any descriptionreferred to in paragraphs (c), (d), (e) and (f) of subsection (2)of section 38 and may engage in such other transactionswith such institutions as are expedient or desirable in thepublic interest and are appropriate having regard to thecharacter of the Central Bank.\n\n(2) Any loan taken as provided in subsection (1) may besecured by assets held by the Central Bank.\n\n(3) The Central Bank may act as an agent or correspondentof a foreign commercial bank or an investment bank or anyinstitution referred to in paragraphs (b), (d), (e) and (f) ofsubsection (2) of section 38.\n\n41.(1) The Central Bank shall manage the officialinternational reserves consistent with international bestpractices and the guidelines issued by the Governing Boardhaving regard to the safety, liquidity and return in that orderof priority.\n\n(2) The international reserves of the Central Bank mayinclude the following assets:\u2013\n\n(a)gold including credit balances representing suchgold;\n\n(b)assets denominated in freely convertible foreigncurrencies in the form of\u2013\n\n(i)foreign notes and coins held by or to the creditof the Central Bank;\n\nLoans to andfrom foreigninstitutions."
